<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="What this personal cultural archive is, and how Film is presented."
>
  <h1>About</h1>
  <h2>A personal cultural archive</h2>
  <p>
    This site is a public presentation of a personal collection. It is
    meant to be read as an archive: objects, context, and discovery.
    It is not a résumé site, a media server, or a streaming service.
  </p>
  <h2>Film is the first collection</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/movies/">Film</a> is published first. Other cultural domains
    may follow later. They will share this site’s shell without being
    forced into a movie-shaped model.
  </p>
  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <p>
    Film titles, synopses, posters, and audience ratings come from
    <a href="https://www.themoviedb.org/">TMDB</a>. They are not original
    cataloguing by this archive. This product uses the TMDB API but is not
    endorsed or certified by TMDB.
  </p>
  <h2>Availability</h2>
  <p>
    “Currently available” on the snapshot means a copy is present in the
    published catalog. It does not mean the film can be played here.
    Unavailable titles remain part of the public catalog; they are not
    treated as broken records.
  </p>
  <h2>Access and interpretation</h2>
  <p>
    Pages are designed to be readable with a keyboard, a screen reader,
    and a range of screen sizes. Metadata is shown when it helps a
    visitor understand a work, not because a database field exists.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
